title: Renal Transplantation
---

# Renal Transplantation

- **Definition** - transplant of donor kidney into recipient's pelvis, form of RRT that has the best cost effectiveness offers the best chance of long-term survival in ESRD
- **Forms of kidney grafts:**
  - Deceased donor kidney graft - after brain death (51%) or rarely circulatory death (11%)
  - Living donor kidney graft (38%)
- **C/I of renal transplantation:**
  - **Absolute contraindication:**
    - <u>Active malignancy</u> - requires period of \>= 2y of complete remission recommended for most tumours
    - <u>Renal pathology</u> - active vasculitis or recent-anti-GBM disease
    - <u>Severe cardiac disease</u> - no survival benefit offered to patients with severe heart disease
    - <u>Aortoiliac vascular disease</u> - comprimised perfusion as donor vessels are typically anastamosed to recipients internal iliac artery
  - **Relative contraindication:**
    - <u>Age</u> - not absolute indications, but transplants are not routinely performed in extremes of age (\< 1y or \> 75y)
    - <u>Disease of lower urinary tract</u> - increased risk of graft non-function by urinary tract obstruction, but an ileal conduit may be considered
    - <u>Risk of disease recurrence to transplant kidneys</u>
    - <u>Significant comorbidity</u>
- **Factors influencing incidence of graft-rejection** - some HLA incompatible living donor transplants are possible but involves pre-transplant plasmapheresis and significant immunosuppression
  - ABO compatibility
  - Degree of matching for MHC antigens (particularly HLA-DR)
- **Immunology of allograft rejection:**
  - **T cell-mediated rejection** - recipient T lymphocyte responding to donor HLA expressed within transplant organ:
    - <u>CD4+ T cell activation</u> - responds to class II (HLA-DR) incompatibility by proliferation and release of proinflammatory cytokines that influence subsequent B cell activation and CD8+ T cell activation
    - <u>CD8+ T cell activation</u> - responds to class I (HLA-A, -B) incompatibility through direct and indirect pathways, in the presence of **co-stimulatory signals** (CD28 and CD80/86) resulting in **activation of calcineurin**, and upregulation of cytokines such as IL-2 and INF-gamma:
      - IL-2 - stimulates target of rapamycin (TOR) and induces cellular proliferation in autocrine fashiion
      - Cell-mediated cytotoxicity - release of granzymes and perforin and attack on target cells
  - **Antibody-mediated rejection** - caused by circulating Ab againsts donor (HLA or non-HLA) Ag:
    - <u>B cell activation</u> - donor derived Ag drain into LN and activate an alloimmune response, resulting in production of criculating Ab (these may be preformed)
    - <u>Follicular helper T cell</u> - role in promoting B cell differentiation into plasma cells
    - <u>Effects of Ab against donor Ag</u> - mediating tissue injury through complement-dependent (peritubular deposition of C4d) and complement independent mechanisms

  
  ![](../img/immunology_of_rejection_kidney_transplant.png)
- **Presensitisation and desensitisation** - existence of Ab against donor Ag before transplant:
  - **Mechanisms of presensitisation** - presence of autoAb against donor Ag (either HLA- or non-HLA Ag) implies previous sensitisation:
    - Blood transfusion
    - Prior transplant
    - Pregnancy
    - Vaccination or infection (less common)
  - **Detection of presensitisation** - ?N.B. does not detect T cell-mediated rejections:
    - <u>Cross-matching of recipient serum w/ donor lymphocytes</u> (by flow-cytometric or cytotoxic crossmatch assays) - donor T lymphocytes used as surrogate for class I HLA (HLA-A and -B); donor B lymphocytes used as a surrogate for class II HLA (HLA-DR and -DQ)
    - <u>Eplet matching</u> - detection of eplet especially in class II HLA-DQ loci known important risk factor of acute rejection
  - **Approach to Mx of presensitisation:**
    - <u>Desensitisation</u> - aimed at reducing level of anti-donor Ab using plasmapharesis +/- administration of IVIG w/ variable success
    - <u>Kidney paired donnation programs</u> - swapping of living kidney programs so each recipient receives compatible transplant organ increasingly used
- **Peri-operative care:**
  - **Perioperative period:**
    - Adequate haemodialysis performed \<= 48h prior to surgery as needed to control serum K and prevent intra-operative and early post-operative cardiac arrhythmia
  - **Details of operations:**
    - Graft kidney placed in recipient iliac fossa using a <u>retroperitoneal approach</u>
    - Anastamosis between donor renal artery and recipient external iliac artery, and between donor renal vein to recipient external iliac vein
    - Donor ureter anastomosed to recipient bladder mucosa
    - Native kidney nephrectomy rarely performed less in cases of extremely enlarged polycystic kidney or chronic pyelonephritis
- **When to expect urine production and return of renal function** - slow recovery or persistent oliguria should prompt allograft Bx to identify superimposed acute tubular injection:
  - Urine production should occur immediately after anastamosis
  - Especially as there is post-operative diuresis due to presence of some degree of acute tubular injury w/ ischaemic time
  - Gradual fall of SCr as allograft starts to function w/ recovery usually occuring within 2 weeks (although may be as long as 6 weeks)

## Immunosuppressive Therapy

- **Indications of immunosuppressive therapy** - indicated for all except:
  - 1\. Living transplant from identical twins
  - 2\. Theoretically selective simultaneous bone marrow-kidney transplant patients
- **Classification of immunosuppressive therapy:**
  - Induction agents (to prevent early rejection; mediating effects in a depleting or non-depleting manner)
  - Maintenance agents (usually a combination of a calcineurin inhibitor and an antimetabolite +/- adjuvant steroid therapy)
- **Induction agents:**
  - <u>Depleting agents</u> - antithymocyte globulin (ATG), alemtuzumab (MAb against CD52)
    - <u>Non-depleting agents</u> - Basiliximab (IL-2R inhibitors prophylaxis of hyperacute rejections)
- **Maintenance therapy:**
  - Usually a combination of <u>calcineurin inhibitor</u> (usu. tacrolimus), and an <u>antimetabolite</u> (usu. MMF) w/ or w/o early steroid withdrawal
  - <u>Co-stimulatory blocking Ab</u> (e.g. Belatacept) used as alternative to long-term toxic CNI therapy
  - <u>mTOR inhibitors</u> (e.g. sirolimus or everolimus) infrequently used as first-line maintenance immunosuppression

  
  ![](../img/maintenance_drugs_for_kidney_transplantation.png)
- **Post-transplant care of the kidney recipient:** 
![](../img/typical_algorithm_for_post-transplant_care_of_kidney_recipient.png)
- **Glucocorticoids:**
  - <u>Indications</u> - used as both induction therapy and maintenance therapy w/ rapid tapering in post-operative period (due to undesirable effects on wound healing)
  - <u>MOA</u> - reduced expression of IL-6 and IL-1 by macrophages
  - <u>Dosing</u>:
    - Induction therapy - MP 250-500 mg immediately before or at time of transplantation w/ rapid dose reduction to 20 mg within a week
    - Maintenance therapy - P5-10 as a rule (consider discontinuation due to undesirable S/E)
- **Azathioprine** - superceeded by MMF due to inferior immunosuppressive effects:
  - <u>MOA</u>:
    - Bioactivation into mercaptopurine, a thioguanine nucleotide
    - Competitive inhibition for synthesis of DNA and RNA and hence T cell proliferation
  - <u>Caution</u>:
    - TPMT and NUD15 testing
    - Caution w/ allopurinol owing to inhibition of xanthine oxidase
  - <u>S/E</u> - bone marrow suppression
- **Mycophenolic acid** - mycophenolate mofetil (MMF) or mycophenolic sodium):
  - <u>MOA</u> - competitive inhibition for synthesis of DNA and RNA, resulting in inhibition of T cell proliferation
  - <u>S/E</u>:
    - GI disturbances (diarrhoea and cramps)
    - Dose-related hepatotoxicity
    - Marrow toxicity (lesser degree than AZA)
- **Calcineurin inhibitors:**
  - **Selection:**
    - <u>Cyclosporine</u> - fungal peptide w/ potent immunosuppressive activity
    - <u>Tacrolimus</u> (FK506) - fungal macrolidew/ similar action as cyclosporine, but enables once-daily dosing due to the presence of an extended-release formulation
  - **MOA** - inhibition of calcineurin pathway, preventing T cell proliferation through release of <u>IL-2</u> and other proinflammatory cytokines (synergistice effect w/ antimetabolites)
  - **Pharmacokinetics** - very narrow therapeutic window and frequent drug-drug interactions (metabolised by P450 C3A enzymes):
    - <u>Increased level of CNI w/ inhibition of C3A enzymes</u> - ABx (e.g. erythromycin), antifungals (e.g. ketoconazole, fluconazole), non-DHP CCBs (ditiazem, verapamil)
    - <u>Decreased levels of CNI w/ activators of C3A enzymes</u> - ASMs (e.g. phenytoin and carbamazepine)
  - **S/E** - severe S/E, most importantly nephrotoxicity:
    - CNI-related nephrotoxicity
    - Gingival hyperplasia (more w/ cyclosporine)
    - Hirsutism (more w/ cyclosporine)
    - Hair loss (more w/ tacrolimus)
    - Post-transplant DM (more w/ tacrolimus)
    - HTN
    - HL
- **mTOR inhibitors:**
  - **Selection:**
    - <u>Sirolimus</u> - fungal macrolide w/ poorer bioavailability
    - <u>Everolimus</u> - better bioavailability
  - **MOA** - inhibition of mTOR signalling pathway, reducing IL-2 mediated paracrine activation of T cells
  - **Indications** - may be preferred in transplant patients that develop malignancies (esp. recurrent skin malignancies):
    - Used in conjunction w/ CNIs as adjuvant immunosuppression
    - Used w/ MMF to avoid use of CNIs
  - **S/E:**
    - Mucositis (including stomatitis)
    - GI disturbance
    - Pneumonitis
    - Poor wound healing (hence less preferable agents during perioperative period)
- **Belatacept:**
  - **MOA** - Fusion protein composed of Fc fragment of human IgG1 Ig and extracellular domain of CTLA-4:
    - Binds to costimulatory ligand on APCs (CD80/86) preventing interaction w/ CD28 on Th cells
    - Results in <u>T cell anergy and apoptosis</u>
  - **Dosing and administration** - monthly IV infusion
  - **Clinical efficacy** - Belatacept Evaluation of Nephrotoxicity and Efficacy as First-Line Immunosuppression Trial (BENEFIT):
    - <u>Improved patient and graft survival</u> compared to patients treated w/ cyclosporine
    - Likely <u>less nephrotoxicity</u>
    - Higher short-term risks of <u>acute rejection</u>

## Complications of Renal Transplantation

- **Peri-operative complications:**
  - <u>-ve fluid balance</u> - patients may be very polyuric in the initial period after return of renal functions, requiring matching of IO
  - <u>Primary graft non-function</u> - causes classified into pre-renal, renal and post-renal:
    - **Pre-renal** - hypovolaemia (-ve fluid balance), vascular occlusion (e.g. occult aorto-iliac disease)
    - **Renal** - ischaemic ATN during storage and trasfer, pre-existing renal damage in kidney graft, hyperacute rejection
    - **Post-renal** - urinary tract obstruction
  - <u>Sepsis</u> - general surgical risk accentuated by uraemia and immunosuppression
- **Long-term complications:**
  - <u>Rejection</u> - risk theoretically highest in early post-transplantation period, but all require life-long immunosuppression
  - <u>Complications of immunosuppression</u> - cardiovascular events, infection, malignancy (50% develop skin malignancy within 15y of transplantation)
- **Major causes of death in the kidney transplant patients:**
  - Cardiovascular events (28%)
  - Infections (18%)
  - Malignancy (17%)
  - **Opportunistic infections in renal transplant recipients** - different causes based on time lag from transplant:
    - <u>Peri-transplant period</u> (\< 1 mo) - wound infections, herpesvirus, oral candidasis, UTIs
    - <u>Early post-transplant period</u> (1-6 mo) - pneumocystis carinii (septrin prophyaxis for 6 mo), legionella, listeria, CMV (gancyclovir prophylaxis), HBV, HCV
    - <u>Late post-transplant period</u> (\> 6 mo) - aspergillus, nocardia, BK virus, herpes zoster, HBV, HCV

    
    ![](../img/common_opportunistic_infections_post_renal_transplant.png)
- **CMV infections** - serious complication after kidney transplantation:
  - <u>Epidemiology and burden</u> - accounts for increased morbidity and mortality in kidney transplant recipients:
    - Most commonly occurs in 1-6 mo post transplant, but may be delayed
    - Accounts for increased morbidity and mortality in kidney transplant recipients
  - <u>Risk factors</u> - all immunosuppressed individuals affected (risk highest theoretically in sero-ve recipients and sero+ve donors)
  - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - extremely variable attributed to the blunted host response:
    - CMV viraemia (to be distinguished from CMV antigenaemia)
    - Systemic manifestations (fever, leukopenia)
    - Tissue-specific manifestations (retinopathy, gastroenteritis, hepatitis)
  - <u>Prophylaxis and Mx</u> - valganciclovir/ gancyclovir
- **BK nephropathy:**
  - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - latent polyomavirus that lies dormant in kidney and urothelial tracts:
    - Activation in the setting of immunosuppression
    - Progressive fibrosis and loss of graft function within 1y in most cases
  - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - rising SCr, +/- oliguria
  - <u>Routine monitoring and prevention</u>:
    - Regular BK viral load testing
    - Judicious reduction of maintenance immunosuppression
  - <u>Dx</u> - renal Bx:
    - Interstitial nephritis
    - Tubulocytopathic changes of BK nephropathy
    - Viral Ag in allografts
  - <u>Mx</u>:
    - Judacious reduction in immunosuppression
    - Combination therapy w/ leflunomide, cidofovir, quinolone (all effective against polyoma helicase)
    - +/- IVIG
- **Chronic graft kidney dysfunction** - despite excellent 1y transplant survival, most will exerience a progressive decline in kidney function over time there after:
  - <u>Etiology of chronic graft kidney dysfunction</u> - likely multifactorial:
    - Chronic active antibody-mediated rejection
    - Recurrent glomerular disease
    - HTN
    - CNI nephrotoxicity
    - Secondary FSGS
  - <u>Ix</u> - renal Bx to differentiate subacute Ab-mediated rejection from recurrent disease or secondary FSGS
  - <u>Mx</u>:
    - Dependent on underlying cause
    - CCBs for systemic and intrarenal HTN thought to have beneficial effects on rate of progressionof chronic allograft dysfunction
- **Malignancies:**
  - 2-3x risk of increased risk of neoplasms compared to age- and gender-matched general populations
  - RCC and urogenital tract cancers related to duration of ESKD
  - Malignancies a/w oncogenic viruses (EBV, HPV, HHV-8, HBV, HCV) and PTLD occur due to degree of immunosuppression
- **Other long-term complications:**
  - <u>Major cardiovascular events</u> - due to high prevalence of CAD and PAD in ESKD patients, further exacerbated by other post-transplant contributory factors such as use of glucocorticoids, tacrolimus, sirolimus, as well as chronic alograft dysfunction
  - <u>Hypertension</u> - caused by 1) native kidney disease, 2) rejection activity in the transplant, and 3) renal artery stenosis, and 4) renal CNI toxicity; best Mx by CCBs which show an improvement of long-term mortality
  - <u>Hypercalcaemia</u> (persistent hyperparathyroidism) - indicate failure of hyperplastic PTH gland to reress, w/ aggravation of glucocorticoids
  - <u>Anaemia</u> - likely due to marrow suppression of immunosuppressive therapy, or GI bleeding w/ steroids (usually normal EPO production)
  - <u>Post-transplant erythrocytosis</u> (8-15%) - increased risk of arterial and venous thromboembolic events, w/ risks lowered by RAAS blockade
  - <u>Mineral and bone disease</u> - most importantly osteoporosis and avascular necrosis, often multifactorial due to pre-existing renal osteodystrophy, persistent hyperparathyroidism, glucocorticoids, and allograft dysfunction
  - <u>Chronic hepatitis</u> - now able to be managed by effective antivirals
